<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toyotomi clan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Toyotomi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toyotomi clan was the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, who unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the late sixteenth century. After his death in 1598 his young heir Hideyori was eclipsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> describe how the Tokugawa eliminated the Toyotomi in the sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1614–1615, removing the last rival to their rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan.jpg" alt="Toyotomi clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: file created on Adobe Illustrator and Photoshop by: Zagyoso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goshichi_no_kiri_inverted.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The destruction of the <b>Toyotomi</b> house in the sieges of Osaka Castle in 1614 and 1615 removed the last plausible rival source of legitimacy and completed the Tokugawa monopoly of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As Tokugawa forces took Osaka Castle, <b>Toyotomi</b> Hideyori and his mother took their own lives.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His daughters, Chacha, Hatsu and Go, later shaped <b>Toyotomi</b> and Tokugawa politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>He began Osaka castle as his base, became imperial regent (kampaku) in 1585 and received the new surname <b>Toyotomi</b> in 1586.</blockquote><a href="/state/toyotomi-regime">Toyotomi regime</a></li></ul></section>
